<div id="HDR">&nbsp;</div>

<DIV STYLE="width: 708px; border-top: 1pt white solid; border-bottom: 1pt white solid; border-right: 1pt white solid; border-left: 1pt white solid; padding-top: 9pt; padding-bottom: 9pt; padding-right: 3pt; padding-left:6pt; margin-top:6pt; margin-right: 0pt; margin-left:0pt; margin-bottom:6pt" ALIGN="CENTER">

<P STYLE="text-align: left; font-family: serif; font-size: 8pt; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: bold"><a href="#TOC">TABLE OF CONTENTS</a></P>

<H3 STYLE="text-indent:0pt; text-align: left; font-family: serif; font-size: 10pt; line-height: 12pt; font-style: italic; font-variant: normal; font-weight: bold; text-transform: none; padding-top: 5pt; padding-right: 0pt; padding-left: 4px; padding-bottom: 3pt; margin-top: 0pt; margin-right: 0pt; margin-left: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt">Medical Innovation </H3>

<P STYLE="text-indent:20px; text-align: left; font-family: serif; font-size: 10pt; line-height: 12pt; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; text-transform: none; padding-top: 3pt; padding-right: 0pt; padding-left: 4px; padding-bottom: 3pt; margin-top: 0pt; margin-right: 0pt; margin-left: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt">According to Marcus and Millichap&nbsp;&#151;&nbsp;<I>The Outlook: Healthcare Reform, A winning prognosis for medical office investments </I>(2009), technology, innovation and research and development will play crucial roles in healthcare reform, providing yet another potential demand driver for medical space. The medical landscape is changing rapidly as new treatments, drugs and devices become available. We believe that such change will result in increased spending on research, including investments in studies in the comparative effectiveness of drugs and treatment, thereby generating demand for research and development facilities and traditional
medical office space. </P>

<TABLE CELLSPACING="0" CELLPADDING="0" WIDTH="100%" STYLE="text-indent:0px; text-align: left; font-family: serif; font-size: 10pt; line-height: 12pt; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; text-transform: none; padding-top: 3pt; padding-right: 0pt; padding-left: 0pt; padding-bottom: 3pt; margin-top: 0pt; margin-right: 0pt; margin-left: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt">

<TR STYLE="font-weight: normal; font-style: normal; line-height: 12pt; font-size: 10pt; vertical-align: top; text-align: left">

          <TD STYLE="width: 21px"></TD>

          <TD STYLE="width: 24px; text-align: left">&#149;</TD>

          <TD STYLE="text-align: left">According to Government Technology.com (2010), the $787 billion American Recovery and Reinvestment Act of 2009, or the ARRA, has allocated approximately $19 billion to facilitate the development of electronic medical records. Additionally, according to the National Institute of Health (2009), the ARRA also contains $10 billion for medical research to the National Institute of Health to be distributed in the form of grants to top researchers around the country for, among other purposes, studies comparing the effectiveness of certain drugs and treatments. As a result, we believe that the ARRA will increase the demand for research and development facilities and traditional medical office space. </TD>

</TR>

</TABLE>

<H3 STYLE="text-indent:0pt; text-align: left; font-family: serif; font-size: 10pt; line-height: 12pt; font-style: italic; font-variant: normal; font-weight: bold; text-transform: none; padding-top: 5pt; padding-right: 0pt; padding-left: 4px; padding-bottom: 3pt; margin-top: 0pt; margin-right: 0pt; margin-left: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt">Recent Performance </H3>

<P STYLE="text-indent:20px; text-align: left; font-family: serif; font-size: 10pt; line-height: 12pt; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; text-transform: none; padding-top: 3pt; padding-right: 0pt; padding-left: 4px; padding-bottom: 3pt; margin-top: 0pt; margin-right: 0pt; margin-left: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt">Despite the recent economic recession, medical properties have performed favorably, and demand is set to accelerate over the next several years as the trends discussed above are realized. Unlike other asset types, medical properties continue to garner investors&#146; demands by exhibiting considerable resistance to the economic downturn. According to Marcus and Millichap&nbsp;&#151;&nbsp;<I>The Outlook: Healthcare Reform, A winning prognosis for medical office investments </I>(2009), medical property vacancy is currently 11.6%, up only 100 basis points from one year ago; by comparison, traditional office vacancy is currently 15.2%, a 240 basis point
increase over the same time period. Further, many medical space users prefer locating on multiservice medical campuses, which we believe could offer a rare opportunity for medical office development in the coming years. </P>

<H3 STYLE="text-indent:0pt; text-align: left; font-family: serif; font-size: 10pt; line-height: 12pt; font-style: italic; font-variant: normal; font-weight: bold; text-transform: none; padding-top: 5pt; padding-right: 0pt; padding-left: 4px; padding-bottom: 3pt; margin-top: 0pt; margin-right: 0pt; margin-left: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt">Acquisition Strategy </H3>

<P STYLE="text-indent:20px; text-align: left; font-family: serif; font-size: 10pt; line-height: 12pt; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; text-transform: none; padding-top: 3pt; padding-right: 0pt; padding-left: 4px; padding-bottom: 3pt; margin-top: 0pt; margin-right: 0pt; margin-left: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt">Carter &amp; Associates has more than 50 years of experience as one of the nation&#146;s top full-service commercial and institutional real estate firms. Carter &amp; Associates has significant expertise in developing and financing healthcare related real estate projects. Carter &amp; Associates provides hospitals and health systems clients with a full spectrum of real estate facility financing and development services, as well as industry expertise in operations, strategic, facility and capital planning, program management, facility ownership and development, leasing and property management. The Carter &amp; Associates team has led approximately $6
billion of healthcare facility planning and development projects across 36 states. Carter &amp; Associates has a track record of long-term client relationships, which often span several decades. Our acquisition strategy will focus on the sourcing of acquisitions, investments and developments through existing relationships of Carter &amp; Associates with healthcare system, company and medical office users, as well as through traditional channels. </P>

<H2 STYLE="text-indent:0pt; text-align: left; font-family: serif; font-size: 10pt; line-height: 12pt; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: bold; text-transform: none; padding-top: 5pt; padding-right: 0pt; padding-left: 4px; padding-bottom: 3pt; margin-top: 0pt; margin-right: 0pt; margin-left: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt">Technology Industry and Data Center Property Sector </H2>

<P STYLE="text-indent:20px; text-align: left; font-family: serif; font-size: 10pt; line-height: 12pt; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; text-transform: none; padding-top: 3pt; padding-right: 0pt; padding-left: 4px; padding-bottom: 3pt; margin-top: 0pt; margin-right: 0pt; margin-left: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt">Data centers are the cornerstone of an emerging communication services industry and the technology real estate class. Demand for quality built &#147;scalable&#148; data centers is at a high level due to favorable market trends. </P>

<P STYLE="text-indent:20px; text-align: left; font-family: serif; font-size: 10pt; line-height: 12pt; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; text-transform: none; padding-top: 3pt; padding-right: 0pt; padding-left: 4px; padding-bottom: 3pt; margin-top: 0pt; margin-right: 0pt; margin-left: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt">A data center is a structure utilized by a company to house and operate its computer operations, including hardware, software applications, communications and all support mechanicals (MEP) necessary to the company&#146;s operation. These specialized facilities typically house technical equipment, such as servers, routers and switches in secure, highly improved and resilient environments that run 24 hours a day, 365 days a year. Data centers are utilized by major corporations in all types of industries. </P>

<P STYLE="text-indent:20px; text-align: left; font-family: serif; font-size: 10pt; line-height: 12pt; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; text-transform: none; padding-top: 3pt; padding-right: 0pt; padding-left: 4px; padding-bottom: 3pt; margin-top: 0pt; margin-right: 0pt; margin-left: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt">Power has become the primary resource required when operating data centers, having eclipsed square footage in importance. In this market space, revenue is directly related to available power and real estate square footage provided to a data center. As a result, higher power-density customer equipment will be hosted and priced at a higher rate. </P>

</DIV>

<P STYLE="width: 708px; text-indent:0px; text-align: center; font-family: serif; font-size: 10pt; line-height: 12pt; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; color: black; text-transform: none; text-decoration: none; padding-top: 12pt; padding-right: 0pt; padding-left: 0pt; padding-bottom: 3pt; margin-top: 0pt; margin-right: 0pt; margin-left: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt">70</P>

<HR STYLE="width: 708px; height: 2px; text-align: center">

<div id="PN" style="PAGE-BREAK-AFTER: always">&nbsp;</div>

<div id="HDR">&nbsp;</div>

<DIV STYLE="width: 708px; border-top: 1pt white solid; border-bottom: 1pt white solid; border-right: 1pt white solid; border-left: 1pt white solid; padding-top: 9pt; padding-bottom: 9pt; padding-right: 3pt; padding-left:6pt; margin-top:6pt; margin-right: 0pt; margin-left:0pt; margin-bottom:6pt" ALIGN="CENTER">

<P STYLE="text-align: left; font-family: serif; font-size: 8pt; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: bold"><a href="#TOC">TABLE OF CONTENTS</a></P>

<P STYLE="text-indent:20px; text-align: left; font-family: serif; font-size: 10pt; line-height: 12pt; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; text-transform: none; padding-top: 3pt; padding-right: 0pt; padding-left: 4px; padding-bottom: 3pt; margin-top: 0pt; margin-right: 0pt; margin-left: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt">The market for data center services has changed dramatically in the last five years. After the &#147;dot.com era&#148; collapsed and the huge investments made for &#147;Y2K&#148; had been completed, demand for data center services decreased considerably. As a result, many companies put off making any capital investments in data centers until there was a demonstrated need for upgrades. This lack of activity caused a pent-up demand for modern data centers in late 2005 when enterprises experienced increased heating/cooling requirements, space shortages in their own facilities and older third-party data centers did not have the infrastructure to support
modern technologies. </P>

<P STYLE="text-indent:20px; text-align: left; font-family: serif; font-size: 10pt; line-height: 12pt; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; text-transform: none; padding-top: 3pt; padding-right: 0pt; padding-left: 4px; padding-bottom: 3pt; margin-top: 0pt; margin-right: 0pt; margin-left: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt">The T5/Carter Data Center Industry Overview (2007) projects an even greater increase in the need for mission critical data centers resulting from the following recent favorable trends in the marketplace: </P>

<TABLE CELLSPACING="0" CELLPADDING="0" WIDTH="100%" STYLE="text-indent:0px; text-align: left; font-family: serif; font-size: 10pt; line-height: 12pt; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; text-transform: none; padding-top: 3pt; padding-right: 0pt; padding-left: 0pt; padding-bottom: 3pt; margin-top: 0pt; margin-right: 0pt; margin-left: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt">

<TR STYLE="font-weight: normal; font-style: normal; line-height: 12pt; font-size: 10pt; vertical-align: top; text-align: left">

          <TD STYLE="width: 21px"></TD>

          <TD STYLE="width: 24px; text-align: left">&#149;</TD>

          <TD STYLE="text-align: left">As technology has advanced and internet use has increased exponentially, data centers developed as recently as ten years ago are virtually obsolete. While processor size has sharply decreased, overall processing capacity requirements have exploded. As a result, the supply of data center space is deeply constrained. </TD>

</TR>

</TABLE>

<TABLE CELLSPACING="0" CELLPADDING="0" WIDTH="100%" STYLE="text-indent:0px; text-align: left; font-family: serif; font-size: 10pt; line-height: 12pt; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; text-transform: none; padding-top: 3pt; padding-right: 0pt; padding-left: 0pt; padding-bottom: 3pt; margin-top: 0pt; margin-right: 0pt; margin-left: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt">

<TR STYLE="font-weight: normal; font-style: normal; line-height: 12pt; font-size: 10pt; vertical-align: top; text-align: left">

          <TD STYLE="width: 21px"></TD>

          <TD STYLE="width: 24px; text-align: left">&#149;</TD>

          <TD STYLE="text-align: left">The constant per bite of data stored or processed is power. Current data center designs contemplate changes in space needs, but more importantly, also include future power demand metrics in their capacity specifications. </TD>

</TR>

</TABLE>

<TABLE CELLSPACING="0" CELLPADDING="0" WIDTH="100%" STYLE="text-indent:0px; text-align: left; font-family: serif; font-size: 10pt; line-height: 12pt; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; text-transform: none; padding-top: 3pt; padding-right: 0pt; padding-left: 0pt; padding-bottom: 3pt; margin-top: 0pt; margin-right: 0pt; margin-left: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt">

<TR STYLE="font-weight: normal; font-style: normal; line-height: 12pt; font-size: 10pt; vertical-align: top; text-align: left">

          <TD STYLE="width: 21px"></TD>

          <TD STYLE="width: 24px; text-align: left">&#149;</TD>

          <TD STYLE="text-align: left">Changes in healthcare technology and data center needs also are expected to drive a need for space proximate to medical facilities. </TD>

</TR>

</TABLE>

<TABLE CELLSPACING="0" CELLPADDING="0" WIDTH="100%" STYLE="text-indent:0px; text-align: left; font-family: serif; font-size: 10pt; line-height: 12pt; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; text-transform: none; padding-top: 3pt; padding-right: 0pt; padding-left: 0pt; padding-bottom: 3pt; margin-top: 0pt; margin-right: 0pt; margin-left: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt">

<TR STYLE="font-weight: normal; font-style: normal; line-height: 12pt; font-size: 10pt; vertical-align: top; text-align: left">

          <TD STYLE="width: 21px"></TD>

          <TD STYLE="width: 24px; text-align: left">&#149;</TD>

          <TD STYLE="text-align: left">Due to security concerns, many companies and government entities prefer, and often require, a single tenant configuration for their data center needs. </TD>

</TR>

</TABLE>

<H3 STYLE="text-indent:0pt; text-align: left; font-family: serif; font-size: 10pt; line-height: 12pt; font-style: italic; font-variant: normal; font-weight: bold; text-transform: none; padding-top: 5pt; padding-right: 0pt; padding-left: 4px; padding-bottom: 3pt; margin-top: 0pt; margin-right: 0pt; margin-left: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt">Acquisition Strategy </H3>

<P STYLE="text-indent:20px; text-align: left; font-family: serif; font-size: 10pt; line-height: 12pt; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; text-transform: none; padding-top: 3pt; padding-right: 0pt; padding-left: 4px; padding-bottom: 3pt; margin-top: 0pt; margin-right: 0pt; margin-left: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt">T5 Partners, LLC and Carter Development Services, L.L.C, a subsidiary of Carter &amp; Associates, formed an alliance on February 29, 2009, which we refer to as T5/Carter, which enables T5/Carter to scale project management services as projects dictate. Since 1995, Carter Development Services has provided specialized site selection, brokerage services, project management and delivery for some of the world&#146;s largest corporations. T5 Partners&#146; alliance with Carter Development Services offers T5 Partners a strong platform and provides deep project management resources required to execute large and complex projects, thus strengthening T5
Partners&#146; proven expertise in mission critical real estate. </P>

<P STYLE="text-indent:20px; text-align: left; font-family: serif; font-size: 10pt; line-height: 12pt; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; text-transform: none; padding-top: 3pt; padding-right: 0pt; padding-left: 4px; padding-bottom: 3pt; margin-top: 0pt; margin-right: 0pt; margin-left: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt">T5/Carter is responsible for delivering more than 10 million square feet of space for over 100 contact and data center projects and is widely recognized as one of the nation&#146;s foremost experts in the mission critical real estate industry. T5/Carter provides data center clients with a full range of real estate services and specific industry expertise, including site selection services, acquisition, disposition, real estate brokerage, capital sourcing, leasing, development and project management. Our acquisition strategy will focus on the sourcing of acquisitions, investments and developments through existing relationships of T5/Carter with data
center users, as well as through traditional channels. </P>

<H2 STYLE="text-indent:0pt; text-align: left; font-family: serif; font-size: 10pt; line-height: 12pt; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: bold; text-transform: none; padding-top: 5pt; padding-right: 0pt; padding-left: 4px; padding-bottom: 3pt; margin-top: 0pt; margin-right: 0pt; margin-left: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt">Educational Industry and Property Sector </H2>

<P STYLE="text-indent:20px; text-align: left; font-family: serif; font-size: 10pt; line-height: 12pt; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; text-transform: none; padding-top: 3pt; padding-right: 0pt; padding-left: 4px; padding-bottom: 3pt; margin-top: 0pt; margin-right: 0pt; margin-left: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt">In these difficult economic times, we believe that educational properties are likely to stand out as strong performers in 2010 and beyond due to the following: </P>

<TABLE CELLSPACING="0" CELLPADDING="0" WIDTH="100%" STYLE="text-indent:0px; text-align: left; font-family: serif; font-size: 10pt; line-height: 12pt; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; text-transform: none; padding-top: 3pt; padding-right: 0pt; padding-left: 0pt; padding-bottom: 3pt; margin-top: 0pt; margin-right: 0pt; margin-left: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt">

<TR STYLE="font-weight: normal; font-style: normal; line-height: 12pt; font-size: 10pt; vertical-align: top; text-align: left">

          <TD STYLE="width: 21px"></TD>

          <TD STYLE="width: 24px; text-align: left">&#149;</TD>

          <TD STYLE="text-align: left">Total enrollment in degree-granting institutions increased from 17.8 million students in 2006 to 18.2 million students in the fall of 2007, and according to U.S. Department of Education, <I>Projections of Education Statistics 2018</I> (September 2009) is projected to rise to 20.6 million students by the fall of 2018. Enrollment is expected to increase in both public and private degree-granting institutions. </TD>

</TR>

</TABLE>

</DIV>

<P STYLE="width: 708px; text-indent:0px; text-align: center; font-family: serif; font-size: 10pt; line-height: 12pt; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; color: black; text-transform: none; text-decoration: none; padding-top: 12pt; padding-right: 0pt; padding-left: 0pt; padding-bottom: 3pt; margin-top: 0pt; margin-right: 0pt; margin-left: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt">71</P>

<HR STYLE="width: 708px; height: 2px; text-align: center">

<div id="PN" style="PAGE-BREAK-AFTER: always">&nbsp;</div>

<div id="HDR">&nbsp;</div>

<DIV STYLE="width: 708px; border-top: 1pt white solid; border-bottom: 1pt white solid; border-right: 1pt white solid; border-left: 1pt white solid; padding-top: 9pt; padding-bottom: 9pt; padding-right: 3pt; padding-left:6pt; margin-top:6pt; margin-right: 0pt; margin-left:0pt; margin-bottom:6pt" ALIGN="CENTER">

<P STYLE="text-align: left; font-family: serif; font-size: 8pt; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: bold"><a href="#TOC">TABLE OF CONTENTS</a></P>

<TABLE CELLSPACING="0" CELLPADDING="0" WIDTH="100%" STYLE="text-indent:0px; text-align: left; font-family: serif; font-size: 10pt; line-height: 12pt; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; text-transform: none; padding-top: 3pt; padding-right: 0pt; padding-left: 0pt; padding-bottom: 3pt; margin-top: 0pt; margin-right: 0pt; margin-left: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt">

<TR STYLE="font-weight: normal; font-style: normal; line-height: 12pt; font-size: 10pt; vertical-align: top; text-align: left">

          <TD STYLE="width: 21px"></TD>

          <TD STYLE="width: 24px; text-align: left">&#149;</TD>

          <TD STYLE="text-align: left">According to the U.S. Department of Education, over the past 15 years student enrollment at U.S. colleges and universities has increased by 3.3 million students. The next 11 years are projected to see 2.3 million additional students, as well as enrollment of younger students increasing at a faster rate. California enrollments remained the highest in the nation from 2000 to 2008, with Texas, New York, Florida and Illinois following in rank. </TD>

</TR>

</TABLE>

<P STYLE="text-indent:0pt; text-align: center; font-family: serif; font-size: 10pt; line-height: 12pt; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; text-transform: none; padding-top: 3pt; padding-right: 0pt; padding-left: 4px; padding-bottom: 3pt; margin-top: 0pt; margin-right: 0pt; margin-left: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt"><IMG SRC="v175022_enrollmenttrends.jpg" ALIGN="MIDDLE" ALT="[GRAPHIC MISSING]"></P>

<TABLE CELLSPACING="0" CELLPADDING="0" WIDTH="100%" STYLE="text-indent:0px; text-align: left; font-family: serif; font-size: 10pt; line-height: 12pt; font-style: normal; font-variant: normal; font-weight: normal; text-transform: none; padding-top: 3pt; padding-right: 0pt; padding-left: 0pt; padding-bottom: 3pt; margin-top: 0pt; margin-right: 0pt; margin-left: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt">

<TR STYLE="font-weight: normal; font-style: normal; line-height: 12pt; font-size: 10pt; vertical-align: top; text-align: left">

          <TD STYLE="width: 21px"></TD>

          <TD STYLE="width: 24px; text-align: left">&#149;</TD>

